My hand contains a mix of ramp, threats, and utility creatures: `Den Protector`, `Gruul Signet`, `Lavalanche`, `Managorger Hydra`, `Mirror Entity`, `Primeval Protector`, `Ravos, Soultender`, and `Stalking Vengeance`.\n\
\n\
**Optimal Play:**\n\
My primary objective is to rebuild my board and find a way to deal with the overwhelming number of creatures on my opponents' side of the table.\n\
\n\
1.  **Ramp:** Casting `Gruul Signet` is the most logical first step. It costs {2} mana and will accelerate my mana development, allowing me to cast my more expensive spells sooner. This is a low-risk, high-reward play that increases my future options. I will tap my `Mountain` and `Swamp` to pay for it.\n\
\n\
2.  **Threat Assessment:** After casting the `Gruul Signet`, I will have three mana remaining. I can use this to cast either `Managorger Hydra` or `Den Protector` face-down.\n\
    *   `Managorger Hydra` will immediately begin to grow as my opponents cast spells, representing a long-term threat.\n\
    *   `Den Protector` offers the utility of returning a card from my graveyard to my hand when it is turned face up. This could be used to retrieve the discarded `Stonehoof Chieftain` in the future.\n\
\n\
**Decision:**\n\
The most statistically sound play is to cast the `Gruul Signet` first to accelerate my mana. After that, deploying the `Managorger Hydra` is the superior choice. It immediately starts to scale and becomes a must-answer threat. While the utility of `Den Protector` is valuable, establishing a board presence that can contend with my opponents' is the more pressing need.\n\
\n\
I will begin by casting `Gruul Signet`. This is the first step in my calculated comeback. The probability of this being the correct initial play is very high.",
